Source 1

URL	https://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/treatment/continuous-glucose-monitors.html
Site Author	Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)
Abstract	
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention explains how continuous glucose monitors help people track blood sugar throughout the day. A small wearable sensor measures glucose in the fluid under the skin and sends updated readings to a phone, receiver, or compatible device. The page describes how CGM readings differ from occasional finger-stick tests and explains features such as trend graphs, alerts, and time in range. It also discusses who may benefit from a CGM, including people with type 1 diabetes, and notes that device costs and insurance coverage can vary. This source is useful because it provides an accessible overview of how CGM technology works and how people use the information in everyday diabetes management.

Source 2

URL	https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/managing-diabetes/continuous-glucose-monitoring
Site Author	National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK)
Abstract	
The National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases describes continuous glucose monitoring as a way to estimate glucose levels automatically throughout the day and night. The article explains the main parts of a CGM: a sensor, a transmitter, and software on a phone, pump, or receiver. It compares real-time monitors with devices that require the user to scan the sensor and explains that CGM records can help people and their health care teams notice patterns over time. The page also discusses alerts for high or low readings and features that may track food, activity, and medication. It is a helpful government health source for understanding CGM functions and common features.
